{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction for conviction of assault, threats, theft, drug activity or human trafficking","requirement":"A landlord may evict when the tenant is convicted of, or found civilly liable for, assault, threats, theft, drug activity or human trafficking against the landlord, family or employees. A three-day Notice to Quit is required; suit is barred after two years.","key_value":"3-day Notice to Quit; suit barred 2 years after conviction or release","coverage_conditions":"Tenants under the Anti-Eviction Act, including a tenant who permits a person known to have been convicted to reside on the premises.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Tenancies covered by the Anti-Eviction Act; includes permitting a known convicted person to reside"},"exemptions":"Does not apply to a tenant allowing a juvenile found delinquent for drug use or possession to reside at the property.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No eviction suit may be brought more than two years after: the juvenile was found to be delinquent; conviction of the person; or after the person’s release from incarceration, whichever is later. A Notice to Quit must be served on the tenant at least three day s prior to filing a suit for (N.J.S.A. 2A-18-61.1(o)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction causes and notice for owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings","requirement":"A tenant of a landlord-occupied two- or three-family dwelling may be evicted only by court order and only for the listed causes, including holding over, nonpayment of rent, disorderly conduct, destruction of the premises or violation of written rules or lease provisions.","key_value":"3 months' notice to quit (at will or year-to-year tenancy); 1 month (month-to-month); 3-day Notice to Quit for disorderly, destructive or rule-violating causes","coverage_conditions":"Landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":3,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"A three (3) month notice to quit must be gi ven if an at will tenancy or year -to-year tenancy exists. A one (1) month notice to quit is required for a month-to-month tenancy and other types of tenancies are entitled to a one (1) month notice to quit.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house eviction causes and notice","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, and give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting an eviction action.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to the County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms, unless a Rooming and Boarding House Act exception applies.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Rooming and boarding houses with unrelated occupants and no private kitchens or bathrooms"},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for eviction law violations after owner-occupancy or withdrawal notices","requirement":"A landlord who gave notice to personally occupy the unit must occupy it for at least six months; a landlord who gave notice of permanently retiring the building from residential use must not use the property residentially for five years.","key_value":"6 months' owner occupancy; 5-year bar on residential use","coverage_conditions":"Landlords who obtained a vacancy by notice of personal occupancy or of permanent removal of the property from residential use.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Landlords who gave notice of personal occupancy or withdrawal from residential use"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Treble damages plus attorney fees and costs; up to $10,000 civil penalty per violation for residential use during the five-year period.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"When a tenant vacates a dwelling unit after having been given notice that the landlord wishes to personally occupy the unit the landlord must occupy the unit for at least six (6) months.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Reprisal prohibited against tenants exercising civil rights","requirement":"A landlord may not take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when the tenant enforces rights, makes a good faith complaint to a government authority, or organizes.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"All residential rentals, including mobile homes"},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"The tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant protections in foreclosure proceedings","requirement":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction in New Jersey; a buyer who wants to personally occupy the property must give the tenant a 90-day notice to quit, and a new owner must notify tenants within 10 business days after the sale.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit; 10 business days to notify tenants of the sale","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants in foreclosed properties; month-to-month tenants and owner-occupied two- and three-family units are not exempt from the 90-day notice.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential rental units in foreclosed properties"},"exemptions":"None stated; month-to-month tenants and two- and three-family owner-occupied units are not exempt from the 90-day notice requirements.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69","quoted_span":"Before a tenant can be evicted due to foreclosure, the landlord must provide the tenant with a 90 -day notice to qui t when the foreclosed property has been purchased by a buyer who wants to personally occupy it as his or her primary residence.","interaction":"Federal law does not preempt any State or local law that provides longer time periods or other additional protections for tenants in foreclosure proceedings.","confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy","requirement":"A converter must notify tenants of their right to file for protected tenancy; qualifying senior citizens, permanently disabled tenants or certain disabled veterans may be protected from eviction for 40 years.","key_value":"40 years' protected tenancy; 60-day filing deadline; income limit of three times county average per-person income or $50,000, whichever is greater","coverage_conditions":"Tenants at least 62 before the conversion recording date, or permanently disabled, or honorably discharged and 60% or more disabled, who lived in the building at least one year before the conversion recording date.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Buildings being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":"Tenants whose family income exceeds three times the average per person income in their county and $50,000.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.22 to -61.39","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 extended protected tenancy","requirement":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 extends protected tenancy to qualified tenants in qualified counties who were not eligible under the Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Tenants in buildings converted or being converted in qualified counties; at present the only qualified county is Hudson County.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Qualified counties; at present only Hudson County"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981”","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
